<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeongjo of Joseon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1752">1752</a>–<a href="/years/1800">1800</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as King Jeongjo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeongjo was the 22nd king of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, ruling from 1776 to 1800. The son of the executed Crown Prince Sado, he continued his grandfather Yeongjo's policy of balancing court factions, founded the royal library <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyujanggak">Kyujanggak</a> to promote scholarship and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwaseong-fortress">Hwaseong Fortress</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwon">Suwon</a>. His reign is seen as the last high point of the dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon.jpg" alt="Jeongjo of Joseon" width="313" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aitadzh Z · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%87oson_Kral%C4%B1_Conqco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jeongjo, whose personal name was Yi San, was the second son of Crown Prince Sado and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-hyegyeong">Lady Hyegyŏng</a>. His elder brother, Crown Prince Uiso, died in infancy. In 1762 Yeongjo had Sado executed by confinement in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> chest, after long conflicts and Sado's years of mental illness. On 21 February 1764 Yi San became the adoptive son of Crown Prince Hyojang and Princess Consort Hyosun by Yeongjo's order, because Yeongjo feared that the heir would be opposed as Sado's son.</p><p>The Noron faction protested that Yi San was the son of a prisoner or of a madman and so unfit to succeed. Between 1762 and 1777 some of its members tried to depose him in favour of his half-brothers. Hong Guk-yeong strongly supported his accession, and Kim Jong-su, though a Noron, also helped him. In 1775 Yi San was made regent, but received no military power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Jeongjo was crowned in the Gyeonghuigung palace on 10 March 1776. His first statement to his officials was that he was the son of Sado, whom the former king had executed. He made his mother a Dowager Queen and spent much of his reign trying to clear his father's name. In 1776 Hong Sang-beom, Hong Kye-neung and other Noron members tried to stage a military coup and assassinate him; he had them executed, along with Prince Eunjeon, Hong In-han and Jeong Hu-gyeom.</p><p>He continued Yeongjo's Tangpyeong policy of balancing political factions, favoured members of the Soron and Namin parties over the Noron as a check on them, and relied on Silhak scholars such as Chŏng Yagyong and Pak Chiwŏn. He founded the Kyujanggak Royal Library to improve scholarship and recruit able officers, and opened government posts to men previously barred by social status. From 1782 he chose officers for the Changyongyeong guard, created in 1785, by competitive examination. He ordered the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwaseong-fortress">Hwaseong Fortress</a>, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. In 1791 he issued the Shinhae Tonggong law, which let anyone sell goods at the market and ended the monopoly of the capital's merchant groups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years Jeongjo arranged the marriage of his second son and successor, Sunjo, to the daughter of Kim Jo-sun of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong-kim-clan">Andong Kim clan</a>. He died suddenly in 1800, at the age of 47, in uncertain circumstances, and speculation about the cause continues. He is buried with Queen Hyoui at the royal tomb of Geonneung. His appointment of Hong Guk-yeong let state affairs be swayed by close retainers, and the Andong Kim marriage tilted power to one side and became the background of the era of sedo politics. In 1899 he was honoured as Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zen">Seon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jeongjo of Joseon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yeongjo (r. 1724–1776) and <b>Jeongjo</b> (r. 1776–1800) tried to balance the factions through a 'policy of impartiality' (tangpyeong), but in the nineteenth century royal in-law families, above all the Andong Kim, dominated government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>King Jeongjo</b> opened a door in the late 18th century, appointing sons of concubines as scholars in his royal library, the Gyujanggak.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Changgyeonggung, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in Changgyeonggung, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.58,127&amp;year=1783" aria-label="Changgyeonggung on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jeongjo of Joseon"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5473 4339h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5473 4339h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.58,127&amp;year=1783">What was here in 1783? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
